6. Programming Requirements
You may hard-code your initial data for this project; user input and a GUI are not required unless a later Change Request explicitly modifies that requirement. Use representations appropriate to each mathematical structure, but deliberately consider what behavior, validation, formatting, testing, or interfaces can be shared.

Required output/testability constraints
Universal set size: Use a universal set with n ≥ 10 elements for Part 1.
Representations: For Part 1 results, display both the bit string / Boolean-array representation and an element-name listing.
Representative runs: Include at least 2–3 representative test cases, not just one.
Edge testing: Include at least one edge case relevant to the operation being tested.
Multiset non-triviality: For Part 2, ensure both A and B include at least two elements with multiplicity > 1.
Labeling: Label outputs clearly so ordinary set operations are not confused with multiset operations or later Change Request functionality.
Regression requirement: After the Week 2 change is implemented, all previously required Set and Multiset functionality must continue to operate correctly.
Part 1 – Ordinary Sets Using Bit Strings / Boolean Arrays
Given subsets A and B of a universal set with n elements, use bit strings or an array of Booleans to find:

NOT(A) – complement of A with respect to the universal set
A ∪ B – union
A ∩ B – intersection
A − B – set difference
A ⊕ B – symmetric difference, defined as (A − B) ∪ (B − A)
Fix and document the ordering of the universal set. For each result, display both the bit-string/Boolean-array representation and a readable element-name listing.

Part 2 – Multisets (Bags)
Given multisets A and B from the same universal set, track a non-negative integer multiplicity for each element and find:

Multiset union (A ∪ B): for each element, take the maximum count in A and B.
Multiset intersection (A ∩ B): for each element, take the minimum count in A and B.
Multiset difference (A − B): for each element, subtract B's count from A's count, but not below 0.
Multiset sum (A + B): for each element, add the two counts.
Label these results clearly as multiset operations so they are not confused with the ordinary set results from Part 1.

